menu "KeyKeeper2"

    config KEYKEEPER_LITE
        bool "Build the Lite variant (strip paid-tier features)"
        default n
        help
            KeyKeeper2 ships as two variants from one codebase, feature-
            flagged rather than kept on a separate branch -- same
            precedent as excluding a whole component (see
            EXCLUDE_COMPONENTS in the top-level CMakeLists.txt for
            components/gui), just applied per-feature instead of
            per-component where a feature doesn't cleanly own its own
            component.

            When enabled, code behind "#if !CONFIG_KEYKEEPER_LITE" (C/
            C++) or "if(NOT CONFIG_KEYKEEPER_LITE)" (CMakeLists.txt) is
            compiled out of the build entirely -- not hidden at
            runtime behind a settings toggle the user could re-enable.
            See profiles/README.md for the exact build commands for
            each variant, and profiles/lite.defaults for the actual
            flag.

            Lite strips (tracked as each is actually wired up, not all
            landing in one commit): the dial-style PIN entry mode,
            on-device Backup, the password generator, display
            rotation, the Wi-Fi captive portal, and PIN hashing
            (reverted to the project's original single-pass salted
            SHA-256, no PBKDF2 stretching). None of these are security
            boundaries between the two variants -- Lite is a feature
            tier, not a reduced-trust build -- except PIN hashing
            specifically, which genuinely weakens offline brute-force
            resistance and is a deliberate, documented trade for a
            free/advertising tier, not an oversight.

endmenu
